The baseline spends one plaintext multiply (with a rescale) and one rotation. The rotation costs about 20 ms, so the saving has to come from the order of operations.

Rotating first and then multiplying by a rotated mask would need a plaintext-plaintext rotation, which is not allowed. The mask is an input, so it can't be pre-rotated at compile time.

The only alternative is to rotate the input first and multiply afterwards. That still needs the mask rotated, so it doesn't work.

The rotation is cheaper at a deeper level, which means multiplying first and rotating after (the baseline order) is already the better order. The multiply and rotate are both required to get the right result, so I see no way to remove either.

One thing that might help is the order of rescale and rotate. After the multiply, the ciphertext is at a higher scale and rescale has not yet been applied, and CHEHAB handles this automatically. The rotation of a lower-level ciphertext is cheaper, so rescaling first is already the best option.

I'll return the baseline, written differently, as the safe answer. It will not beat the baseline because the circuit is unchanged.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  Ciphertext masked = input * mask;
  Ciphertext output = masked << offset;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

The idea is to rotate the input first and apply the mask pre-rotated by the same offset. The output is the same as (input·mask) rotated, but the circuit has a different shape. If CHEHAB allows rotating a plaintext input, that rotation is done at encoding time and costs no ciphertext rotation. This may fail to compile, in which case I'll fall back.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  Plaintext m2 = mask << offset;
  Ciphertext output = (input << offset) * m2;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

Rotating a plaintext is rejected, and the mask can't be pre-rotated. The remaining cheapest form is multiply, then rotate, which is the baseline. As a last variation I'll express the rotation as a right rotation by `slots() - offset`, which is mathematically identical and may lower differently.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  Ciphertext masked = input * mask;
  Ciphertext output = masked >> (static_cast<int>(slots()) - offset);
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I don't have a cheaper valid circuit, so this is the baseline kernel unchanged.

The output needs one mask multiply and one rotation, and I found no way to remove or cheapen either:

- **Rotating a plaintext:** attempt 2 showed CHEHAB rejects it as plaintext-plaintext evaluation, so the mask can't be pre-rotated.
- **Rotating first:** the unrotated mask would then be applied at the wrong slots. The same rotation would also run at the top level, where it costs more.
- **Skipping the mask:** slots outside the window would then hold wrapped-around input values instead of zeros.
- **Splitting the rotation:** two smaller rotations would cost more than one.

Multiply then rotate is the cheapest ordering because the rotation runs after the rescale, at a reduced level. Attempt 3 showed that rewriting the rotation direction leaves the circuit identical, so I'm not spending further attempts on rewrites.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  Ciphertext output = (input * mask) << offset;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I still can't find a valid circuit with fewer operations than the baseline, so I'm resubmitting it unchanged.

Every correct form needs `input * mask` followed by a rotation by `slots()/4`. The mask is a plaintext input that can't be rotated, and the zeros outside the window can only come from multiplying by it. Reordering the two operations moves the rotation to a higher level, where it costs more. I'd rather keep a correct, known-timed kernel than submit a variant that changes the result.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  Ciphertext output = (input * mask) << offset;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I have nothing new to try. Every valid form of this kernel needs one plaintext multiply and one rotation, and the baseline already does the rotation at the cheapest point, after the rescale. Rotating first gives wrong values because the mask can't be rotated to match. I'm submitting the baseline kernel again, since a different circuit would have to be incorrect or more expensive.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  Ciphertext output = (input * mask) << offset;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

There is no cheaper correct circuit, and I'm not going to submit a wrong or slower one just to differ from the baseline. The baseline already uses the minimum: one plaintext multiply, one rescale and one rotation done at the reduced level.

```cpp
